<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The whole manifest for a one-activity, zero-permission, offline game.

  == PERMISSIONS: THERE ARE NONE, AND THAT IS A DECISION ==

  Not even INTERNET. Gameplay is entirely offline - the 845 voice clips are
  staged into the APK's assets, the roster is SharedPreferences, and the economy
  is arithmetic. The two things that would need a socket are both switched off
  in this build:

    * household SYNC is not wired at all (A7: ProfileStore is built with
      sync = null, because the services/api contract is being reworked);
    * TELEMETRY is compiled in and inert, because it is inert without an
      endpoint and no endpoint is declared below.

  So the app opens no socket, and a permission it does not use would be a
  permission we would have to justify on the Play Data safety form and in a Kids
  Category review for nothing. ManifestContractTest pins the coupling in BOTH
  directions: no endpoint may be declared without INTERNET (every telemetry send
  would throw SecurityException and be swallowed, which is the worst of both
  worlds), and no permission may be declared while no endpoint is. Turning
  telemetry on is therefore a two-line change, and the test names the second
  line.

  To enable an endpoint, add BOTH of these inside application, plus the INTERNET
  permission above it. The meta-data keys are the same names the iOS app uses in
  its Info.plist, so the release runbook is one page for two stores:

      meta-data android:name="ALTelemetryURL" android:value="https://..."
      meta-data android:name="ALSyncURL"      android:value="https://..."

  PlatformConfiguration.fromMetaData reads them; both are optional and both fail
  silent when absent, which is what makes "absent" the safe default a build that
  forgot them gets.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      allowBackup and the two rules files are A5, and they are load-bearing
      rather than boilerplate. Play has no price-0 in-app product, so unlike iOS
      there is no signed StoreKit purchaseDate to date the 14-day trial from:
      the stamp is a local value in SharedPreferences, and Auto Backup is the
      only thing that carries it across a reinstall. Both rules files include
      the whole sharedpref domain and exclude exactly one file, the device id -
      a counter key must belong to exactly one LIVE device, or max()-merged
      counters read two devices' independent progress as one stale one and a
      child loses stars (invariant 9). :platform's SharedPreferencesKVStoreTest
      asserts the excluded filename is the same string the code opens.

      android:name is the process's own class: it builds the object graph and
      primes the SFX tracks before a window exists, which is what "audio is
      warm before the first tap" means in practice (invariant 1).
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".AttrapeLettresApplication"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="false"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.AttrapeLettres">

        <!--
          ORIENTATION. The game is authored portrait: the card caps at 480 dp
          and every fluid size is a fraction of the viewport WIDTH, so landscape
          buys nothing and costs the vertical room the exercise grids need. The
          platform caveat is noted rather than pretended away - an app targeting
          SDK 36 has its orientation lock IGNORED on displays 600 dp and wider,
          so on a tablet or an unfolded foldable this attribute is a request the
          system may decline. That case still renders correctly (the shell
          top-centres a 480 dp card at any width and each screen scrolls
          itself); it is simply not the composition the art was drawn for.

          SCREEN SIZES are deliberately unconstrained - no compatible-screens,
          no supports-screens. Those are Play distribution filters, and a filter
          that hides the game from a family's tablet is a worse outcome than a
          roomy layout on it.

          configChanges is an ACTIVITY-LIFETIME decision, not laziness. The
          object graph is process-scoped (see AppGraph) but the ROUTE is a plain
          mutableStateOf inside RootView, so an activity recreation throws a
          six-year-old out of the round they were in. Every entry listed is a
          change Compose re-reads by itself - density and fontScale reach
          LocalDensity through the composition locals, the rest are pure layout
          - so handling them costs nothing and keeps the child where they were.

          windowSoftInputMode=adjustResize pairs with enableEdgeToEdge() in
          MainActivity: together they are what make WindowInsets.safeDrawing
          report the IME, which is what keeps the « Ton prénom » field and the
          parental gate's answer box above the keyboard.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|density|fontScale|keyboardHidden|uiMode"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.AttrapeLettres">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>

</manifest>
